// PIN (RED — waiting on a spelling, not on a fix): `std/io:read-file` allocates
// its result with `koru_allocator()` (io.kz:189, `file.readToEndAlloc`) and hands
// it back as a bare `| ok string` (io.kz:174). The caller below prints it and
// drops it. The buffer lives until process exit and no pass says a word, because
// the branch payload carries no phantom for the checker to hold on to.
//
// The machinery is NOT missing. An obligation on a primitive `string` already
// works — 335_047 declares `-> string<tainted!>` and the checker tracks it
// through a discharge and now through a stale read. What is missing is the
// SPELLING on the IO surface: what the allocation label is called, and what the
// disposer that consumes it is called.
//
// THE QUESTION FOR LARS (do not answer it by inventing one):
//   1. `~pub tor read-file { path: string } | ok string<???!> | …`
//      — what is the label? `allocated`, `owned`, something else?
//   2. What consumes it? A new `std/io` tor taking `<!???>`, or does
//      `std/string:free`-shaped ownership already own this and IO should hand
//      back a `*String<view!>` instead of a bare `string`?
//   3. `read-stdin`, `read-line`, and every other `readToEndAlloc` path in
//      io.kz have the same hole (335_043 is the stdin twin) — one label for all
//      of them, or per-tor?
//
// EXPECT stays `CONTAINS allocated` as the provisional pin. It moves to whatever
// Lars spells, and only then.
//
// Grounding (verified 2026-07-31):
//   read-stdin decl      — koru_std/io.kz:150
//   read-file decl       — koru_std/io.kz:173, `| ok string` at 174
//   the allocation       — koru_std/io.kz:189
//   obligation on a bare `string` works — 335_047/input.k:39

~import std/io

// Read a file and use its content, then drop it. The allocator-owned buffer
// leaks; no phantom exists on the payload, so nothing refuses.
~std/io:read-file(path: "data.txt")
| ok content |> std/io:print.ln("{{ content:s }}")
| not-found |> _
| failed _ |> _
